  "# ADR-2607198100: Evidence-gated security gap and score register

## Context

Security posture had been described across repository-specific threat models,
but there was no single machine-readable answer for zero trust, capabilities,
PQC, HSM, recovery, transport attacks, impersonation, ABAC, authorized abuse,
supply-chain tampering, key theft, memory corruption, DoS, information flow,
insiders, unknown compromise, quantum communication and unrecoverable loss.
This made scores easy to overstate and gaps hard to close reproducibly.

## Decision

`kotoba-lang/security/registers/stack-security-score.edn` is the authoritative
assessment register for the eight repositories. Every repository must contain
exactly the same 18 controls and each score must be an integer from 0 through
100. Scores are derived into averages; averages are not hand-maintained.

Every gap has an owner scope, state, acceptance criteria and evidence links.
Writing an ADR, roadmap, test vector or provider interface does not by itself
raise an implementation score. A gap may be marked `:resolved` only after its
acceptance criteria have executable or operational evidence. Hardware and live
operations claims such as HSM non-exportability, paging and geo-restore cannot
be closed by a unit-test substitute.

QKD/quantum-network transport is an explicit non-goal. It remains scored zero
and must not be conflated with the portable PQC hybrid migration track.

## Immediate remediation

The aiueos decision subprocess test now invokes its declared Clojure entry
point instead of the nonexistent `bb decide` task. The exact kotoba-lang and
core-contracts commits are publicly reachable, and standalone dependency
preparation and the non-development test path pass without local overrides.
Consumer repositories pin the shared security library by immutable git SHA.
The security CI also
enforces the key-register gate so accidental private PEM material cannot pass
the assurance repository's normal workflow.

The four-axis ABAC evaluator is common code in `kotoba-lang/security`, not a
copy in each product. aiueos deployment policy, compiler capability admission,
kototama transport admission and kagi disclosure governance now normalize
their local attributes into that evaluator and fail closed at their boundary.
Kotoba package admission and kotobase remote-store XRPC admission use the same
decision schema; denied kotobase operations never reach the injected transport.

The security library also owns the classification lattice and downgrade gate.
Kagitaba applies labels to every field, kagi requires exact expiring grants for
reveal downgrades, and compiler admission rejects implicit output downgrades.
Kototama transport writes and kotobase XRPC writes apply the same gate before
bytes or mutations reach their injected network transports.
Aiueos deployment decisions carry the flow evidence, while kotoba host effects
reject before provider invocation and emit a denial receipt with that evidence.
The shared redactor qualifies structured and textual secret removal before the
aiueos audit sink. Covert and side-channel exclusions are explicit non-claims.

Kototama native TLS can require a client certificate, verifies that the local
certificate chain was actually presented, records the decision, and refuses
to issue a TLS handle on missing-certificate or endpoint-mismatch paths.
Aiueos and kotobase use the common transport profile to require TLS 1.3,
mutual authentication, exact peer identity, trusted fingerprints, revocation
checks, validity windows and a staged next certificate before network use.
The common certificate state machine executes stage, overlap, promotion and
old-certificate revocation, then proves rollback to the revoked certificate is
denied. Its EDN receipt explicitly remains simulated-CA rather than production
CA/HSM evidence.

The resilience library hash-links remote telemetry and runs a destructive
restore against two independently identified encrypted backup sites. Its EDN
receipt records digest verification and measured RTO/RPO while explicitly not
claiming production-region independence.

## Consequences

The current averages remain the conservative baseline because governance
artifacts do not erase cryptographic or operational gaps. Future score changes
must edit the register, satisfy its validator, cite new evidence and update the
gap state in the same review."}]
